   "accessed": "2026-11-25",
   "note": "Dimensions and outlines read off 2.2.2's side, plan and front views (tools/trace/b753/), starting from the 757-200's points (tools/trace/b752/) moved by the two plugs 2.7.1's door table and the wheelbases give (4.064 m between door 2 and the wing, 3.048 m between the wing and the aft cargo door) and snapped onto this sheet, which is drawn 3.6% larger than 2.2.1; the figures are the aircraft's measurements, not a copy of the drawing. Plain wingtips (the document has no winglet sheet; United's and Delta's 757-300s fly with blended winglets, which this shape doesn't have) and the PW2000's nacelle (as on the 757-200's sheet; Condor's and Icelandair's RB211s are longer). The sheet draws the cabin windows; the seven behind the wing's lines are placed at its own pitch. Doors 1, 2, 3 (the Type I exit behind the wing), 4 and the cargo doors sit within 0.1 m of 2.7.1's table. As on the 757-200's sheet the side view draws the fuselage 4.22 m tall against the printed 4.01 m (2.2.1's figure: 2.2.2 prints no height; its plan's width is as printed): fit to it (fit_height.py; the check's +1.4% is the wing-body fairing). The front view draws the nacelle 2.35 m across where the sheet prints 2.46 m (RR) and 2.70 m (PW); kept the drawing's."
  }
